{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_ebhmuuduantcbpmbi5dsohmtelaiwfd3tig3xianqnb2apfgtmda","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`StaticAlias` is tiny but nastier than it looks: two exported functions where `static_alias` owns a **process-lifetime `static int inner = 1`** and returns a pointer that is *either* `&inner` *or* the caller's own pointer.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n- [x] **`SYMBOLS.md`** — C exports exactly `driver` + `static_alias`; Rust exports both with identical names. 0 missing, 0 unresolved non-libc imports. Verified for debug *and* release.\n- [x] **Phase B** — all 24 `CONFIGS.md` rows pass across randomized inputs (fixed seed).\n- [x] **Phase C** — all 11 `ERRORS.md` rows have passing differential tests.\n- [x] **Every configuration** — `Cargo.toml` declares **no `[features]`**, so there is exactly 1 combination; I additionally verified the release profile (`panic=\"abort\"`, debug-assertions off) and the C library rebuilt at `-O2`/`-O3`.\n\n## The real bug found (Phase C, rows E1/E2)\n\nA happy-path-invisible divergence in fault behavior:\n\n| | `static_alias(NULL)` | `static_alias(0x1)` |\n|---|---|---|\n| C | `SIGSEGV` (11) | `SIGSEGV` (11) |\n| Rust before | **`SIGABRT` (6)** | **`SIGABRT` (6)** |\n| Rust after | `SIGSEGV` | `SIGSEGV` |\n\nRust 1.78+ emits a *debug-assertion* validity precondition on raw-pointer derefs, so `*outer` turned the hardware fault into `null pointer dereference occurred` → non-unwinding panic → abort. Every std accessor has some precondition (`read_volatile` skips the null check but enforces alignment; `read_unaligned` is the reverse), so I routed the caller-pointer load/store through `extern \"C\"` libc `memcpy` — check-free at every opt level. `inner`, being the library's own always-valid static, still uses a direct access.\n\n## Two methodology notes worth flagging\n\n- **A stale `.so` nearly hid the fix.** With `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` only, integration tests can't link the crate, so `cargo test` leaves the previous `libStaticAlias.so` on disk — and the tests `dlopen` that file. My first fix appeared to fail for this reason. `run_diff_tests.sh` now forces `cargo build` first.\n- **The hidden static can't be reset,** so I built `probe_inner` (passing `INT_MIN` takes the else branch and reveals `inner` *without mutating it*) and `set_inner` (doubling to wrap to 0, up to `INT_MIN`, then one call to land on any target) — both using only the public API. This is what made states like `inner == 0`/`INT_MAX`/`INT_MIN` reachable. Both libraries always receive identical call sequences, keeping their private copies in lockstep.\n\nI also confirmed the suite isn't vacuous: 5 injected mutants (`>=`→`>`, wrong return aliasing, `inner` starting at 0, `\"%d\\n\"`→`\"%d \"`, loop `<`→`<=`) were all caught, and `src/lib.rs` was restored byte-identical afterward. `c_src/` is unmodified apart from the instructed `build/` directory."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1233,"cache_read_input_tokens":142363,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1233,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"223a0e3a-c943-4c1c-85c1-326e2197a590","uuid":"cf6b0181-5d2f-45f3-9c09-de55d0a47262","timestamp":"2026-08-17T23:13:19.191Z"}
